The main problem is that this is an unsafe DOM sink:

```js
function renderHTML(userInput) {
  document.getElementById('output').innerHTML = userInput;
}
```

## Issues

### 1. DOM XSS vulnerability

If `userInput` contains attacker-controlled content, setting it via `innerHTML` allows HTML injection and potentially JavaScript execution.

Example payloads:

```html
<img src=x onerror="alert(document.cookie)">
```

```html
<a href="javascript:alert(1)">Click me</a>
```

```html
<svg onload="alert(1)"></svg>
```

Even if some browsers do not execute `<script>` tags inserted with `innerHTML`, many other vectors still work, such as event handlers, dangerous URLs, SVG payloads, malicious iframes, etc.

### 2. Missing null check

If there is no element with ID `output`, this throws:

```js
Cannot set properties of null
```

because `document.getElementById('output')` can return `null`.

### 3. Unexpected type coercion

If `userInput` is `null`, `undefined`, an object, etc., assigning it to `innerHTML` may produce unexpected strings like `"null"`, `"undefined"`, or `"[object Object]"`.

### 4. Replacing `innerHTML` destroys child state

Setting `innerHTML` removes existing child nodes and recreates them. This can remove event listeners, form state, focus state, and other DOM state inside `#output`.

---

## Safer fix if you only need to display text

Use `textContent`, not `innerHTML`.

```js
function renderOutput(userInput) {
  const output = document.getElementById('output');

  if (!output) {
    throw new Error('Missing #output element');
  }

  output.textContent = userInput == null ? '' : String(userInput);
}
```

This treats the input as text, so this:

```html
<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>
```

will be displayed literally instead of being parsed as HTML.

---

## Safer fix if you really need to render limited HTML

Use a well-maintained sanitizer such as DOMPurify. Do not try to sanitize HTML with regex.

```js
function renderHTML(userInput) {
  const output = document.getElementById('output');

  if (!output) {
    throw new Error('Missing #output element');
  }

  const dirty = userInput == null ? '' : String(userInput);

  const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {
    USE_PROFILES: { html: true }
  });

  output.innerHTML = clean;
}
```

Even better, use DOMPurify to return a DOM fragment:

```js
function renderHTML(userInput) {
  const output = document.getElementById('output');

  if (!output) {
    throw new Error('Missing #output element');
  }

  const dirty = userInput == null ? '' : String(userInput);

  const fragment = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {
    USE_PROFILES: { html: true },
    RETURN_DOM_FRAGMENT: true
  });

  output.replaceChildren(fragment);
}
```

If your app only allows a small subset of tags, explicitly whitelist them:

```js
const fragment = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {
  ALLOWED_TAGS: ['b', 'i', 'em', 'strong', 'p', 'ul', 'ol', 'li', 'a'],
  ALLOWED_ATTR: ['href', 'title'],
  RETURN_DOM_FRAGMENT: true
});
```

---

## Additional security improvements

- Add a Content Security Policy as defense in depth.
- Consider Trusted Types to prevent accidental unsafe `innerHTML` usage.
- Keep DOMPurify updated.
- Prefer building DOM nodes manually with `createElement()` and `textContent` when possible.
- Avoid passing raw user input into any of these sinks: `innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, `insertAdjacentHTML`, `document.write()`.

Recommended default version:

```js
function renderOutput(userInput) {
  const output = document.getElementById('output');

  if (!output) {
    throw new Error('Missing #output element');
  }

  output.textContent = userInput == null ? '' : String(userInput);
}
```

Only use sanitized HTML rendering if there is a genuine requirement to support user-provided HTML.
